{
 "_comment": "Tuition and fees as a share of an institution's total revenue (IPEDS finance, FY2023-24), against the temporary-visa share of its graduate enrolment (GSS 2024). THE ANSWER SPLITS BY SECTOR AND MUST BE QUOTED THAT WAY: among public institutions the more tuition-dependent half runs a HIGHER foreign graduate share, among private institutions a LOWER one. The opposite signs are themselves the argument against reading either causally. PUBLIC AND PRIVATE ARE NEVER POOLED: they report on different accounting standards, GASB and FASB, with line items that are not interchangeable -- public tuition is net of discounts while the private revenue total includes investment return. THE TUITION FIGURE IS FROM EVERY STUDENT, not from foreign students: IPEDS does not report revenue by citizenship and nothing here claims it does. The question is whether tuition-dependent institutions run higher foreign enrolment, NOT how much any group paid. Foreign shares are pooled within each half rather than averaged, so a large programme counts for more than a small one.",
 "sources": {
  "finance": "NCES IPEDS Finance, FY2023-24 (F1A public, F2 private)",
